WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.633 --> 00:00:08.167
Today I'll be explaining the metatags [Spoken Word] and [Spoken Verse], which add spoken dialogue to your songs.

00:00:08.167 --> 00:00:14.983
In pop song and other music, there are sometimes parts where the singer suddenly starts speaking in a normal voice in the middle of a song.

00:00:14.983 --> 00:00:20.733
These metatags I'm explaining today create that effect, turning part of the lyrics into spoken dialogue.

00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:25.633
[Spoken Word] is just what the English phrase suggests - an instruction to "speak words" rather than sing them.

00:00:25.633 --> 00:00:30.583
[Spoken Verse] also matches its English meaning - it's an instruction to create a "spoken part" in the song.

00:00:30.583 --> 00:00:36.133
If you're wondering what the difference is... well, let's start by listening to some examples.

00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:41.667
Using these metatags is simple. You just place them before the part you want to be spoken.

00:00:41.667 --> 00:00:48.167
For today's examples, I'll put the spoken part between the first [Chorus] and the [Bridge] .

00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:52.567
I've chosen "Dark, Anime" as the style. making a darker anime song theme.

00:00:57.367 --> 00:01:05.150
First, let's try [Spoken Word] and hear what kind of voice it creates.

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:24.733
Japanese has a unique accent pattern, so the spoken dialogue can sound a bit unnatural.

00:01:31.983 --> 00:01:39.767
Now let's try [Spoken Verse]. Can you tell the difference from [Spoken Word]?

00:01:50.683 --> 00:01:58.467
The difference between [Spoken Word] and [Spoken Verse] might be a bit difficult to notice.

00:01:58.467 --> 00:02:06.250
I'll explain in more detail later, so don't worry if you couldn't hear the difference.

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:15.367
So you've heard both [Spoken Word] and [Spoken Verse].

00:02:15.367 --> 00:02:20.833
To really understand the differences between these two metatags, you might need to hear various examples.

00:02:20.833 --> 00:02:26.767
[Spoken Word] simply speaks the dialogue normally - it's like not matching the dialogue to the music.

00:02:26.767 --> 00:02:32.783
[Spoken Verse] considers the music when delivering the dialogue - it has the effect of matching the dialogue to the music.

00:02:32.783 --> 00:02:38.700
Specifically, it changes the voice quality, adds effects, and puts pauses in the dialogue.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:45.217
The effects vary depending on the music genre, which makes them difficult to understand and explain.

00:02:45.217 --> 00:02:51.400
And what makes it more complicated is that [Spoken Word] and [Spoken Verse] have quite different success rates.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:57.117
Using these two metatags doesn't guarantee that the specified part will become spoken dialogue.

00:02:57.117 --> 00:03:04.517
[Spoken Word] has about an 80% success rate, while [Spoken Verse] has around 50%.

00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:10.850
[Spoken Word] has the higher success rate, but [Spoken Verse]'s additional effects are also appealing.

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:16.633
Next, let's listen to some examples to understand the additional effects of [Spoken Verse].

00:03:16.633 --> 00:03:22.600
Let's try "chillwave", "bossa nova", and "death metal" - genres where the differences should be more noticeable.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:33.983
In "chillwave", strong effects are applied to the vocals to enhance the "chill" elements.

00:03:40.783 --> 00:03:48.567
Even with [Spoken Word], which doesn't match the music, you can still hear some influence from the chillwave style.

00:03:53.333 --> 00:04:01.117
That's quite a whisper voice... it's the voice quality change effect of [Spoken Verse].

00:04:04.667 --> 00:04:12.450
A bit of singing element crept in there - this is a common issue with [Spoken Verse].

00:04:16.833 --> 00:04:24.617
With the relaxed rhythm of "bossa nova", let's see how the spoken part turns out.

00:04:30.483 --> 00:04:38.267
[Spoken Word] doesn't match the music, so the dialogue can sound disconnected and unnatural.

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:49.883
[Spoken Verse] follows the rhythm of the song, with pauses in the dialogue to create a relaxed speaking style.

00:04:55.533 --> 00:05:03.317
This is a case where it completely turned into singing - a failure. Though I don't entirely hate how it turned out...

00:05:07.417 --> 00:05:15.200
The reason I chose "death metal" is obviously to experiment whether it can produce a death growl.

00:05:17.433 --> 00:05:25.217
Unfortunately, [Spoken Word] didn't result in a death voice.

00:05:28.733 --> 00:05:36.517
It seems [Spoken Verse] can't do death voice either... or so I thought, but then - a shout!

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:48.683
This one uses a death voice from the beginning. I wish it was a bit more "death-y" though.

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:58.383
I think you now understand the various effects that [Spoken Verse] can produce from these examples.

00:05:58.383 --> 00:06:04.533
If you didn't notice much difference, you might be better off using just [Spoken Word] with its higher success rate.

00:06:04.533 --> 00:06:10.650
If you found [Spoken Verse] appealing... you'll have to live with its lower success rate.

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:16.917
Most failures result in the dialogue sounding more like singing, so if you're okay with that, feel free to use it.

00:06:16.917 --> 00:06:21.900
Finally, let me add two more points about success rates.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:28.467
If you place these metatags in the intro - the beginning of the song - the success rate drops dramatically.

00:06:28.467 --> 00:06:34.083
If you want to add dialogue at the beginning of a song, it might be better to use Replace Section.

00:06:34.083 --> 00:06:40.483
After generating the song, you can recreate just the first 30 seconds using Replace Section for better results.

00:06:40.483 --> 00:06:46.333
The other issue is with music genres - certain styles have dramatically lower success rates.

00:06:46.333 --> 00:06:52.333
If you generate multiple times and the dialogue never works, it's probably impossible with that style.

00:06:52.333 --> 00:06:58.133
There is a way to address this issue. Add "spoken word" to the style as well.

00:06:58.133 --> 00:07:03.200
The "spoken word" style helps turn the vocals into dialogue.

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:08.933
However, it tends to make the entire song dialogue-like... the side effect is significant and can make it difficult to use.

00:07:08.933 --> 00:07:14.833
Combined with the "Audiobook" style, which means narration, you can turn the entire song into dialogue.

00:07:14.833 --> 00:07:20.833
I'll explain the "spoken word" style in more detail in another video.

00:07:20.833 --> 00:07:26.950
Interestingly, the style shows its true power in instrumental tracks rather than creating dialogue effects.

00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:30.233
That's all for today - see you next time!

